1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,972
Welcome to Episode 2 of Hello World, pg_bio!

2
00:00:03,972 --> 00:00:07,944
In Episode 1, we treated DNA and proteins as 1D strings.

3
00:00:07,944 --> 00:00:11,916
But in reality, a protein folds into a 3D molecular machine where catalysis happens

4
00:00:11,916 --> 00:00:15,888
inside a 4-Angstrom binding pocket.

5
00:00:15,888 --> 00:00:20,856
Normally, finding which atoms sit inside a 3D sphere requires pulling millions of coordinates

6
00:00:20,856 --> 00:00:25,824
into Python and building an in-memory KD-tree or running brute-force Pythagorean distance loops.

7
00:00:25,824 --> 00:00:30,720
pg_bio solves this with Morton coding—also known as a Z-Order space-filling curve.

8
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,616
Our Rust function z_order_encode interleaves the bits of X, Y, and Z coordinates into

9
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:40,512
a single 64-bit integer. That means a standard 1D PostgreSQL B-Tree index now acts

10
00:00:40,512 --> 00:00:45,408
as a 3D spatial bounding box!

11
00:00:45,408 --> 00:00:51,480
Even better, once the B-Tree narrows down the pocket atoms, theozyme_rmsd runs a zero-allocation

12
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:57,552
3-by-3 Jacobi SVD right inside SQL to rotationally align the catalytic metal and nucleophilic water.

13
00:00:57,552 --> 00:01:03,240
That is 3D structural biology powered by B-Trees and linear algebra inside PostgreSQL.

14
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:08,928
Visit ideia dot me and the pg_bio GitHub repo to try the interactive 3D notebooks.
